all right let's do some audience  questions this week's questions are  really good first one is from Li Li says  do you have a frightening childhood  experience that that has affected you  into adulthood there's one in particular  that I can think of which is I generally  don't like medical stuff I don't like  videos of medical procedures I don't  like any of it and I remember being  seven or eight years old and walking by  the living room and this was when TLC  The Learning Channel I don't know if  they used to be the Learning Channel now  it's just TLC or vice versa anyway that  channel now it's a lot of reality shows  and like house programs and stuff like  that it used to be they used to show  medical procedures and surgeries and  once my dad was watching some kind of  eye procedures and eye surgery and it  freaked me out so bad that I remember  getting very nauseous and it wasn't  something I was supposed to see I just  kind of walked by I think it was later  at night I came out of my room or  something like that and when I think  about I don't know if that was actually  the catalyst when I think about now my  just disc distaste for any medical stuff  and just anxiety around medical issues  and stuff like that  I think back to that and I think back to  visiting my grandfather in the hospital  after he had had a relatively minor  heart attack from what I recall and just  really hating the whole vibe and I know  most people probably don't like  hospitals or medical facilities but I  think that for me it's something that  has really affected me as an adult still  I've actually undergone two pre major  surgeries and I've offered to show you  the video clips for them in the past but  you're just not willing to see them  there they're not even that invasive I  have no interest in that do you want to  say what the procedures were not so much  okay all right well we'll talk about  that later  okay so yeah I mean that I certainly  remember that another one that I sort of  remember is when I got to the United  States and going to school and I didn't  speak any English so I I couldn't  understand what anybody was saying and I  think that although now I'm perfectly  comfortable traveling to countries where  I don't understand the language and I  sort of find it interesting to spend  three months on duolingo a rosetta stone  trying to get some understanding of a  new language I still remember  the sort of feeling of not knowing what  the hell is going on which for me in  great part was because I didn't speak  the language well I think now you're  just in a very good position knowing  both English and Spanish because those  are both very international languages  that if people aren't native speakers of  they may have that as their second  language so sure in a very good position  especially to go to places like Europe  yeah I mean and not only that because  even people who don't speak English in  Europe if you go to Italy or France or  Portugal you're going to have a very  easy time if you speak Spanish fluently  in at minimum figuring out what the  signs say and having you know basic  conversation particularly in Italy and  Portugal yeah and if that doesn't work  you can always resort to drawing  pictures or something else yeah I  actually have friends who when they went  to China they got drawings not not  drawings characters Chinese characters  so that they could hold them up to  communicate certain things like they  were vegetarian so they had signs which  were like I am vegetarian please bear in  mind that even if you give me just  vegetables it might be cooked in meat  broth and the meat broth makes it not  vegetarian which ended up being super  useful because they would go to a lot of  restaurants and were it not for the  clarification in Chinese characters that  somebody wrote up for them they would  have been in a real trouble you know  when I was in Portugal I tried to go to  this breakfast place and tried to order  eggs because it was a few years ago  before I was vegan and all else and so  first I used the English word hoping  they would know the word eggs didn't  work then I moved on to the Spanish word  thinking you know they're right next to  Spain Davos they should be able to  understand that yeah also didn't work I  just left the place that wasn't gonna  deal with yeah there's another thing  which is that I've been told that for  historical reasons in Portugal they  don't like it when you try to speak in  Spanish to them so when I went there  even though I knew we could easily  communicate if I spoke Spanish and they  spoke Portuguese when I couldn't  communicate in Portuguese I would just  speak English because I was told that's  sort of considered off-putting if you  look at a map of which states are more  bilingual in Europe it's really not  places like Portugal or Spain or England  or Scotland it's more of a central part  of Europe yeah all right well in any  case those are my thoughts on that

